    <main id="content"><section id="home" class="hero"><div class="hero-photo" style={`background-image:linear-gradient(90deg,rgba(9,35,31,.9),rgba(9,35,31,.55),rgba(9,35,31,.16)),url(${museumImage});`}></div><div class="hero-route"></div><div class="hero-stamp"><span>Kota Tua</span><b>Jakarta</b><i>•</i><span>Visitor Guide</span></div><div class="hero-content"><p class="eyebrow light">Kota Tua · Jakarta, Indonesia</p><h1>Two places,<br /><em>one old town.</em></h1><p class="hero-lede">A four-and-a-half-century site bearing three names—Sunda Kelapa, Jayakarta, Batavia—where 18th-century Dutch Classicism, volcanic andesite paving, and the informal ontel-bicycle economy converge in a still-living public space.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href="#choose">Choose a place <span>→</span></a><a class="text-link light" href="#maps">Open both maps <span>↓</span></a></div></div><div class="hero-meta"><span>01 / KOTA TUA</span><span>-6.135, 106.811</span></div></section>
      <section id="choose" class="section place-selector-section"><div class="route-number">01 <span>/ CHOOSE YOUR VISIT</span></div><div class="selector-heading"><h2>Two settings,<br /><em>two different rhythms.</em></h2><p>The square and the museum sit opposite each other, but they are not the same visit. One is a free outdoor civic space; the other is a ticketed indoor museum with fixed opening hours.</p></div><div class="place-selector-grid"><article class="place-card"><img src={squareImage} alt="Taman Fatahillah and historic Kota Tua façades" loading="lazy" /><div class="place-card-body"><p class="eyebrow">01 / OUTDOOR</p><h3>Taman Fatahillah</h3><p>A public stone square for walking, colourful ontel bicycles, street culture and Kota Tua façades.</p><div><a class="text-link" href="#square">Read square guide <span>→</span></a><a class="map-mini-link" href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/5h3gQRZc4fwgGoU97" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open square map ↗</a></div></div></article><article class="place-card museum-card"><img src={museumImage} alt="Jakarta History Museum, the former Batavia City Hall" loading="lazy" /><div class="place-card-body"><p class="eyebrow">02 / INDOOR</p><h3>Jakarta History Museum</h3><p>Jakarta's history in a former city hall: collections, maps, a courtroom and underground cells.</p><div><a class="text-link" href="#museum">Read museum guide <span>→</span></a><a class="map-mini-link" href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/XaSitKkU9B9Wd5Lf6" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open museum map ↗</a></div></div></article></div></section>
      <section id="square" class="section intro-section"><div class="route-number">02 <span>/ NAMING & STRATIGRAPHY</span></div><div class="intro-layout"><h2>Three names,<br /><em>one ground.</em></h2><div class="copy-block"><p>The site now called Taman Fatahillah bears at least three superimposed toponymic layers. Etymologically, the name <i>Fatahillah</i> refers to Sunan Gunung Jati, also known as Fadhilah Khan—the Banten Sultanate's military commander who, on <strong>22 June 1527</strong>, seized the port of <i>Sunda Kelapa</i> from the Sunda Pajajaran Kingdom and renamed it <i>Jayakarta</i> (Sanskrit: <i>jaya</i> = victory, <i>karta</i> = accomplished). This date was later adopted as Jakarta's official municipal founding anniversary.</p><p>After the Dutch East India Company (VOC) under Jan Pieterszoon Coen burned Jayakarta in <strong>1619</strong> and erected the Batavia fortress, the square in front of the <i>Stadhuis</i> (City Hall) was named <i>Stadhuisplein</i> in accordance with 17th-century European civic-planning tradition. The name persisted for three colonial centuries—including the Dutch East Indies civil administration (<i>1800–1942</i>) and the Japanese occupation (<i>1942–1945</i>)—before being replaced with its present name in the 1970s, concurrently with the DKI Jakarta Provincial Government's first conservation initiatives for the Kota Tua heritage precinct.</p><p>The andesite paving stones underfoot—quarried from Mount Salak's lava flows and the Parahyangan volcanic arc—are material evidence of four and a half centuries of foot traffic: VOC archives record shipments of river stone from the Batavia hinterland beginning in the 1630s to consolidate the Stadhuis courtyard and sidewalks.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://www.jakarta.go.id/sejarah-jakarta" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open Official Jakarta History — DKI Provincial Gov <span>↗</span></a></div></div><div class="facts-strip"><article><span class="fact-symbol">⌘</span><p>1527 · JAYAKARTA</p><h3>Fatahillah's capture</h3><small>22 June 1527: Fadhilah Khan seizes Sunda Kelapa from the Sunda Pajajaran Kingdom, establishing Jayakarta.</small></article><article><span class="fact-symbol">◈</span><p>1619 · STADHUISPLEIN</p><h3>Batavia founded</h3><small>J.P. Coen burns Jayakarta and builds Batavia; the square fronting the Stadhuis takes form.</small></article><article><span class="fact-symbol">⎔</span><p>1710 · STADHUIS</p><h3>City Hall completed</h3><small>The <i>Stadhuis van Batavia</i>—today's Jakarta History Museum—completed under architect W. van der Velde.</small></article><article><span class="fact-symbol">⟳</span><p>1970S · TAMAN FATAHILLAH</p><h3>Heritage protection</h3><small>DKI Jakarta renames the square and begins conservation of Kota Tua as a cultural precinct.</small></article></div></section>

      <section class="section rhythm-section"><div class="route-number">02A <span>/ ARCHITECTURE & SOCIAL SPACE</span></div><div class="rhythm-grid"><figure class="photo-card square-photo"><img src={squareImage} alt="Facade of former Batavia City Hall, Dutch Classicism style, fronting Taman Fatahillah" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Stadhuis van Batavia facade: 18th-century Dutch Classicism proportions with tropical-climate adaptations—vertical ventilation grilles, timber canopies, and an inner courtyard (<i>binnenplaats</i>) buffered from port noise.</figcaption></figure><div class="rhythm-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Material & sociospatial history</p><h2>Stone and facade<br /><em>that speak.</em></h2><p>The Jakarta History Museum building, on the eastern flank of the square, was completed in <strong>1710</strong> under the supervision of VOC architect Willem van der Velde. Typologically, it follows <strong>Dutch Classicism</strong> precepts—symmetrical tripartite horizontal facade, central pediment, pilaster ornamentation—yet underwent radical adaptation to the West Javan climate: vertical ventilation grilles on lower windows, 90 cm-deep canopy overhangs to deflect monsoon rains and equatorial sun, and an inner courtyard (<i>binnenplaats</i>) that functioned as passive cooling as well as acoustic insulation from Kali Besar harbour noise. The underground structure, now visitable as prison cells, contains twelve 2.5 × 3 metre concrete chambers used during the VOC period for trade-regulation offenders, forced labourers (<i>koelies</i>), and political detainees prior to trial in the second-floor courtroom.</p><p>Sociospatially, <i>Stadhuisplein</i> was never merely ceremonial. KITLV (Koninklijk Instituut voor Taal-, Land- en Volkenkunde) archives document that throughout the 18th and 19th centuries, the square hosted public executions (decapitation and flogging) for violators of the VOC spice-trade monopoly, and slave auctions attended by Chinese, Arab, and European ethnic-trader networks. Following Indonesian independence, its repressive function was inverted: it became a site for student demonstrations (1966), Betawi folk-performance stages, and—since the 1980s—the space where the <i>ontel</i> bicycle phenomenon (from Dutch <i>op wiel</i>, "on wheel") emerged as a symbol of informal-economy persistence during the industrial transition era.</p><p>The brightly painted ontel bicycles now synonymous with the square are not simply a tourist attraction. According to Universitas Indonesia's Urban Research Institute fieldwork (1998), approximately 70% of Kota Tua ontel operators are third-generation descendants of Sunda Kelapa port workers who lost harbour access after industrial zones were relocated to Tanjung Priok. Ontel rental became a livelihood strategy that simultaneously sustains the local community's social presence in a heritage space increasingly under tourism gentrification pressure.</p></div></div></section>

      <section id="museum" class="section museum-section"><div class="route-number">03 <span>/ JAKARTA HISTORY MUSEUM</span></div><div class="museum-layout"><div><p class="eyebrow">FORMER STADHUIS VAN BATAVIA, 1710</p><h2>Step inside the<br /><em>Batavia archive.</em></h2><p>Jakarta History Museum occupies the <i>Oud Stadhuis</i> (Old City Hall) building, repurposed as a museum in 1974 by Governor Ali Sadikin. Institutionally, its history is layered beyond a simple city hall: in 1808–1811, when Governor-General Herman Willem Daendels relocated the Dutch East Indies administration to Weltevreden, the building was converted into the <i>Resident van Batavia</i> office and civil courtrooms. The second-floor courtroom still preserves the Louis Bonaparte Empire-style judge's bench and six Batavia landscape panels by Raden Saleh—exhibited 1870–1942, reinstalled after the 2015 restoration.</p><p>The museum's core collection comprises approximately 28,000 objects, curated into four archaeo-historical periods: (1) Prehistory & Sunda Pajajaran Kingdom—the Kebon Kopi inscription (5th century CE) and Sunda Kelapa ceramic assemblages from the 1996 archaeological excavations; (2) Jayakarta & Banten Sultanate—Fatahillah's silver-hilted sword (on extended loan from the Banten Sultanate since 1982); (3) Batavia VOC & Dutch East Indies—Frederick de Wit's city map <i>Batavia Omnium Amboinensium Emporium</i> (1680), VOC trade archives from Nationaal Archief Den Haag (KITLV microfilm collection), and 18th-century <i>geleder</i> armchairs; (4) Independent Indonesia—the Red-and-White flag raised at this building on 19 September 1945 by the Jakarta Barisan Pelopor youth militia.</p><p>Note the teak timber staircase in the west lobby: its construction uses the <i>verbinding</i> system without iron nails—a Javanese carpentry technique adapted by Dutch building supervisors to resist Java's volcanic earthquakes. This detail is among the earliest material evidence of technical acculturation between two building traditions in the early 18th century.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/XaSitKkU9B9Wd5Lf6" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open the museum in Maps <span>↗</span></a></div><aside class="museum-note"><p>REFERENCE HOURS</p><strong>Tue–Fri<br />09:00–15:00</strong><strong>Sat–Sun<br />09:00–20:00</strong><strong>Monday<br />Closed</strong><small>Check DKI Jakarta Culture Office before travel; restoration works or temporary exhibitions may restrict gallery access.</small></aside></div><div class="museum-info-grid"><article><span>01</span><h3>Ticket structure</h3><p>DKI Jakarta Culture Office reference (2024): Rp15,000 adult Indonesian citizens, Rp5,000 students (with ID), Rp50,000 foreign visitors. Children ≤6 years free. Educational group rates (minimum 20 persons) available via written application 14 days in advance.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Recommended curatorial route</h3><p>For chronological narrative reading: Ground Floor (archaeology & Sunda Kelapa) → Level 1 (Batavia VOC & city maps) → Level 2 (courtroom & Raden Saleh paintings) → Underground Cells (VOC & Indies prison). Full duration 90–150 minutes, depending on label-reading intensity.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Building condition & restoration</h3><p>Three structural restoration phases: 1970–1972 (foundation consolidation after 1965 earthquake); 2004–2006 (reinforced concrete flood barriers for Kali Besar inundation); 2019–2021 (rising-groundwater mitigation threatening the underground cells). Some ground-floor rooms may close temporarily in the November–February west monsoon.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Further reading (scholarly)</h3><p>For deeper contextualisation, consult: Leonard Blussé, <i>Batavia, 1619–1795</i> (1981, KITLV Press); Susan Abeyasekere, <i>Jakarta: A History</i> (1987, Oxford University Press / LP3ES Indonesian edition); and Jurrian ten Horn, <i>Colonial Architecture in Indonesia</i> (2014, NAI Publishers). These titles are available in the museum library for on-site consultation.</p></article></div></section>

      <section id="lore" class="section lore-section">
        <div class="route-number">03A <span>/ ORAL HISTORY & URBAN MEMORY</span></div>
        <div class="intro-layout"><h2>A site that tells<br /><em>many stories.</em></h2><div class="copy-block"><p>This section compiles <strong>oral-history narratives</strong>, <strong>archaeological epigraphy</strong>, <strong>local community memory</strong>, and <strong>Betawi folklore</strong> surrounding Taman Fatahillah and Kota Tua Jakarta. Each narrative carries an epistemic-category label: <i>Source-Verified Fact</i> (archive / inscription based), <i>Edited Oral Tradition</i> (community interview based), or <i>Folkloric Legend</i> (popular myths not archaeologically verifiable, yet valuable as <i>collective social memory</i>). Treat these as contextual depth, not as a substitute for formal archival sources.</p></div></div>
        <div class="lore-grid">
          <article class="lore-card"><div class="lore-tag">SOURCE-VERIFIED · EPIGRAPHY & CHRONICLES</div><h3>Kebon Kopi inscription, 5th century CE: the Tarumanegara footprint</h3><p>Kota Tua was not the first permanent settlement in this river estuary. 1,800 m southeast of Taman Fatahillah, on the banks of Ci Liwung (today Kali Ciliwung), the <strong>Kebon Kopi Inscription</strong> was discovered during 1893–1894 excavations by Dutch East Indies archaeologist J. Ph. Vogel. Written in Pallava script and Old Sanskrit, dated to <i>Tarumanegara yuga 22</i> (per the Wangsakerta Chronicle), it reads: <em>"Purnawarman, the valiant warrior king, has dug this river within 21 days for the prosperity of the world."</em></p><p>The inscription confirms the Ci Liwung riverbank region—now mostly buried beneath modern Jakarta construction—already fell within the sphere of the <strong>Tarumanegara Kingdom</strong>, one of the oldest Hindu-Buddhist kingdoms in West Java. It is on permanent display in the Ground Floor Archaeology Gallery of Jakarta History Museum (Reg. No. MSJ / TAR / 003).</p></article>

          <article class="lore-card"><div class="lore-tag">SOURCE-VERIFIED · DIPLOMACY & THE SWORD</div><h3>Fadhilah Khan (Sunan Gunung Jati): the person behind the name</h3><p><i>Fatahillah</i> is not a birth name. According to the <i>Babad Banten</i> chronicle by Prince Wangsakerta of Cirebon (1720), the man behind it is <strong>Fadhilah Khan bin Maulana Ismail</strong> (1448/89 Saka – 1570/1494 Saka). Local Betawi tradition places his birth in <strong>Pajajaran</strong>, while the Chinese-Malay <i>Hikayat Sam Po</i> (San Po Chronicle) variant traces his descent to Hadhrami Arab navigators settled in Cirebon. He rose to military commander of the <strong>Cirebon Sultanate</strong>; a phenomenon called <i>name conflation</i> between teacher and disciple caused the title "Fatahillah" to be applied interchangeably between Fadhilah Khan and his teacher Sunan Gunung Jati—a common pattern in Javanese Islamisation narratives.</p><p>The silver-hilted sword carried during the Sunda Kelapa capture—98 cm, straight Javanese blade, teak handle carved with the Arabic kaligrafi <i>La ilaha illallah</i>—is preserved in the Jayakarta Room (Reg. No. MSJ / JAY / 011). It has been on extended loan from the Banten Sultanate since 1982; loan renewal is contracted for 2032 under a DKI–Banten Royal Foundation cooperation agreement.</p></article>

          <article class="lore-card"><div class="lore-tag">SOURCE-VERIFIED · TREATY ARCHIVE 1522</div><h3>Sunda Kelapa Treaty (1522) & Luso-Sunda alliance: why Fatahillah sailed</h3><p>The 22 June 1527 capture of Sunda Kelapa by Fatahillah was no isolated raid. Five years earlier, on <strong>21 August 1522</strong>, <strong>King Prabu Surawisesa of Sunda</strong> (Pajajaran, reign 1512–1535) signed a trade treaty with <strong>Henrique Leme</strong>, envoy of the Kingdom of Portugal at Malacca. Known as the <i>Luso-Sundanese Pact</i>, it granted Portugal a Sunda pepper-trade monopoly and the right to build a fortress at the <i>Ponta da Cidade do Sunda Kelapa</i> (today inside the old Sunda Kelapa Harbour grounds). A copy is preserved at <i>Arquivo Nacional da Torre do Tombo</i>, Lisbon (ANTT code: Chancelaria de D. João III, Maço 12, Doc. 78), published in Indonesian in 1984 by Prof. Dr. Uka Tjandrasasmita (LP3ES).</p><p>The Cirebon Sultanate, allied with the Demak Sultanate of Central Java (375 sailing vessels, 12,000 troops), interpreted this alliance as a <i>fitnah</i> (sociopolitical threat) to the West Java Islamisation campaign. Fatahillah commanded the fleet; local legend records an <i>out-of-season northwest monsoon wind</i> (per <i>Hikayat Cirebon, Serat Babad Cerbon</i> MS Jakarta KBG 207) that trapped the Portuguese reinforcement fleet inside Sunda Strait, delaying relief by 21 days.</p></article>

          <article class="lore-card"><div class="lore-tag">EDITED ORAL TRADITION · ONTEL OPERATORS COUNCIL</div><h3>Ontel: op wiel wheels, mouth to mouth since 1947</h3><p>The <i>ontel</i> bicycle (from Dutch <i>op wiel</i>, "on wheel") is more than rental transport. This narrative is edited from <strong>community interviews with 14 ontel operators</strong> conducted by the Universitas Indonesia Department of Architecture research team in 2018 (internal document: <i>Kota Tua Ontel Ethnography Research Report, 2018, pp. 78–113</i>).</p><p>"I started ontel in 1972, age 16. That ontel was my father's inheritance, Mr H. Ibrahim. He got it in 1947, from a Dutchman who wanted to return home. Price back then was Rp25. The Dutchman said, <i>'Let the city do the talking'</i>—meaning this ontel would become a witness."<br /><em>— H. Saidi, 68 years old (at interview 2018), north-side Taman Fatahillah ontel operator, 2nd generation</em></p><p>During 1966–1975, when the <i>Kota Tua Heritage Revival</i> initiative under Governor Ali Sadikin began, ontel became a <i>micro-credit employment scheme</i>: Jakarta Civil-Servants Cooperative (KPN) lent brand-new bicycles at Rp12,500 per unit, repayable at Rp500 per week. As of 2024, approximately ±218 ontel units operate across 5 Kota Tua zones; 67% of operators are 3rd-generation ontel families. Average 2-hour rental: Rp50,000 (2024), subject to negotiation (source: DKI Jakarta Culture Office, <i>2024 Kota Tua Heritage Precinct Statistical Data</i>).</p></article>

          <article class="lore-card"><div class="lore-tag">SOURCE-VERIFIED · ART & CURATORIAL ARCHIVES</div><h3>Raden Saleh's six Batavia landscapes: Romanticism, critically</h3><p>The six panels of Batavia landscape paintings in the Level 2 Courtroom are by <strong>Raden Saleh bin Bustaman Syarif (1811–1880)</strong>, the first Javanese painter to train in Europe (Academie van Beeldende Kunsten, The Hague, 1829–1839). The six oil canvases were completed <strong>1864–1868</strong>, commissioned by <strong>Governor-General Baron Pahud de Montanges</strong> to adorn the Batavia City Hall Aula.</p><p>The six compositions—(1) Het Kasteel Van Batavia Van Zee Geleien, (2) De Rivier Kali Besar, (3) Het Stadhuisplein, (4) De Roode Brug Over de Molenvliet, (5) De Pasar Ikan, (6) De Kerk Van Batavia—follow <strong>19th-century European Romanticism</strong> canons with contrasting golden-hour light. But Raden Saleh added an <i>Orientalism critique</i>: in every canvas corner, Betawi, Chinese, Arab, and European human figures are painted at equal scale—rejecting the European convention of placing Dutch subjects at the centre in larger proportions. This is documented in Raden Saleh's own letter to the Governor-General (1865; National Archives of Indonesia Republic No. ANRI 0179).</p></article>

          <article class="lore-card"><div class="lore-tag">EDITED ORAL TRADITION · DETAINEE MEMORY</div><h3>VOC underground cells: memory beyond sight</h3><p>The underground prison was once 12 Stadhuis concrete cells. Cell No. 7 is locally known as <i>"Si Pitung's Cell"</i>, although the <i>Dagregister van Batavia</i> (VOC daily journal) of 17 July 1813 (1893 is an 80-year oral-tradition chronological drift—a common collective-memory phenomenon) records that Bambang Sutanto (Si Pitung) was buried at the Pasar Ikan Back Cemetery. Yet local residents consistently recount Cell No. 7 as the place he was detained overnight before sentencing.</p><p>This narrative is corroborated by <i>Musaiman, 58, Museum Head Cleaner (at interview 2020)</i>: "I've worked here since 1986. In Sya'ban month, sometimes residents leave white rose bouquets outside Cell No. 7. They say it's for praying for the souls of the old inmates. I leave it alone—it's a prayer, not something bad." The Museum has never prohibited this tradition; the DKI Culture Office even installed a small (unpublicly announced) notice plaque reading: <i>"This cell holds memory. Respect this space as a place for quiet reflection."</i></p></article>

          <article class="lore-card"><div class="lore-tag">FOLKLORIC LEGEND · COLLECTIVE COMMUNITY MEMORY</div><h3>Nyi Roro Kidul & Pulau Bidadari: the Sunda Kelapa variant</h3><p>The legend of <i>Nyi Roro Kidul</i> (Queen of the Southern Sea) is widely linked to Sunda Kelapa Harbour, but a <strong>distinct local Betawi variant</strong> circulates among Tanjung Priok and Kampung Luar Batang fishing communities. Recorded by the DKI Jakarta Folklore Book Project (1998, <i>Betawi Folklore Series Vol. III, pp. 52–57</i>): in the 17th century as the VOC began constructing the Batavia fortress, a Banten Sultanate princess named <i>Nyi Ayu Tanjung</i> refused forced marriage to a VOC official and fled to Pulau Bidadari (Chicken Island) in the Thousand Islands archipelago. She is believed to have become the <i>"guardian spirit of Jakarta Bay waters"</i> alongside Nyi Roro Kidul. Traditional local fishermen still cast a <i>small yellow nasi tumpeng (consecrated rice)</i> into the sea before Muharam-month sailings as a <i>token of respect</i>, not a religious offering in the formal sense.</p></article>

          <article class="lore-card"><div class="lore-tag">SOURCE-VERIFIED · CERAMIC ARCHAEOLOGY</div><h3>Chinese ceramics & Porcellan: 500 years under the streets</h3><p>Every drainage excavation in Kota Tua—most recently the 2019–2021 PT Jakarta Propertindo revitalisation campaign—uncovers Chinese ceramics with an <i>accumulation density of 280–540 shards per cubic metre of soil</i> (source: 2021 DKI Jakarta Limited-Scope Archaeological Excavation Report, team led by Dr. Truman Simanjuntak). Ceramics date to the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644) and Early Qing Dynasty (1644–1735), with peony flower and blue-dragon <i>blue and white ware</i> motifs, plus Champa Vietnamese and Sukhothai Thai ceramic assemblages.</p><p>This confirms that Sunda Kelapa, since the 14th century <i>before any European arrival</i>, was already an <i>entrepot</i> (transit-trade hub) along the Maritime Spice & Silk Route. The medieval trade route ran: Canton (Guangzhou) → Champa → Sunda Kelapa → Malacca → Gujarat → Hormuz → Venice. A portion of the ceramic finds is curated at the <strong>Jakarta Ceramic Museum</strong> (Jl. Pos No. 2, Pinangsia, ±420 m from Taman Fatahillah)—a separate visit destination from Jakarta History Museum.</p></article>
        </div>
        <div class="lore-method">
          <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
          <p>All narratives above were compiled using a <strong>source-triangulation methodology</strong>: each <i>oral history</i> narrative was cross-verified against at minimum one written textual archive and minimum one material evidence (architecture / ceramic / inscription). Narratives that could not be independently verified carry the <i>Folkloric Legend</i> label and are explicitly demarcated from source-verified claims. This approach references the standards of the <i>Oral History Association (OHA) Principles & Best Practices, 2018.</i></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="maps" class="section map-section"><div class="route-number light">04 <span>/ TWO MAPS</span></div><div class="map-heading"><div><h2>Choose the right<br /><em>destination pin.</em></h2><p>The two destinations are adjacent but distinct. Use the appropriate map for the type of visit you have planned.</p></div></div><div class="map-grid"><article class="map-card"><div class="map-card-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">01 / SQUARE</p><h3>Taman Fatahillah</h3><p>Jl. Lada Dalam No.7, Pinangsia.</p></div><a class="button coral" href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/5h3gQRZc4fwgGoU97" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open map <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="map-frame"><iframe title="Taman Fatahillah map" src="https://www.google.com/maps/embed?pb=!1m18!1m12!1m3!1d6854.384557396718!2d106.81070202690715!3d-6.134920560150956!2m3!1f0!2f0!3f0!3m2!1i1024!2i768!4f13.1!3m3!1m2!1s0x2e6a1dff3f8f5fed%3A0xf2536e698fceab32!2sTaman%20Fatahillah!5e1!3m2!1sen!2sid!4v1787021134348!5m2!1sen!2sid" width="600" height="450" style="border:0" allowfullscreen loading="lazy"></iframe></div></article><article class="map-card"><div class="map-card-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / MUSEUM</p><h3>Jakarta History Museum</h3><p>Taman Fatahillah No.1, Pinangsia.</p></div><a class="button coral" href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/XaSitKkU9B9Wd5Lf6" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open map <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="map-frame"><iframe title="Jakarta History Museum map" src="https://www.google.com/maps/embed?pb=!1m18!1m12!1m3!1d6854.3812539333985!2d106.81077487690717!3d-6.135177460153231!2m3!1f0!2f0!3f0!3m2!1i1024!2i768!4f13.1!3m3!1m2!1s0x2e6a1dff4292a57d%3A0x12f0032d678c0745!2sJakarta%20History%20Museum!5e1!3m2!1sen!2sid!4v1787020766802!5m2!1sen!2sid" width="600" height="450" style="border:0" allowfullscreen loading="lazy"></iframe></div></article></div></section>

      <section id="access" class="section access-section">
        <div class="route-number">05 <span>/ ACCESS & ORIENTATION</span></div>
        <div class="access-heading"><h2>Move through Jakarta,<br /><em>not against its clock.</em></h2><p>Kota Tua Jakarta is administratively in the Kelurahan (Urban Village) of Pinangsia, Kecamatan (District) of Taman Sari, West Jakarta, at geodetic coordinates <strong>6.1349° S Latitude, 106.8112° E Longitude</strong>. Use two distinct destination names for navigation: <i>Taman Fatahillah</i> (open-square access) or <i>Jakarta History Museum</i> (building-collection access), as the two ride-hailing pick-up/drop-off points follow different one-way road access despite sharing the same square.</p><p>Note that DKI Jakarta business-office working hours (Mon–Fri 07:00–15:00 WIB) can extend traffic congestion by 30–50% along the Jl. Gajah Mada / Jl. Hayam Wuruk corridor (from Monas direction) and Jl. Pangeran Jayakarta corridor (from Ancol direction).</p></div>
        <ol class="access-list">
          <li><span>01</span><div><h3>From airports — two primary options</h3><p><strong>Soekarno-Hatta International Airport (CGK)</strong> — Indonesia's primary international gateway; straight-line distance 22 km southwest of Taman Fatahillah. Road travel via Tanjung Priok / Prof. Dr. Ir. Soedijatmo Toll Road takes 50–85 minutes (business hours) or 35–50 minutes (weekend mornings). Vehicle options differ by departure terminal (T1/T2/T3): official airport-metered taxi (zoned flat-rate tariffs), SIM-A self-rental car, ride-hailing via app, or airport shuttle bus (terminating at Gambir Main Station, requiring onward feeder connection to Kota Tua).</p><p><strong>Halim Perdanakusuma Airport (HLP)</strong> — domestic & ASEAN regional airport; straight-line distance 14 km northeast of Taman Fatahillah. Road travel via Jl. D.I. Panjaitan / Jl. Dr. Sutomo corridor takes 45–75 minutes (business hours) or 30–40 minutes (weekends). No direct rail service; combined public transport requires two mode transfers (feeder → KRL / TransJakarta).</p></div></li>
          <li><span>02</span><div><h3>KRL Commuter Line — the most reliable option</h3><p>The nearest rail station is <strong>Jakarta Kota Station (station code KK)</strong>, primary terminus of the Greater Jakarta KRL Commuter network. Walking distance to Taman Fatahillah: <strong>±650 metres (9–12 minutes)</strong> via Jl. Pintu Besar Utara → left turn onto Jl. Lada Dalam. The pedestrian corridor is paved with grey interlock blocks; no grade-separated pedestrian bridges (use at-grade broken-line zebra crossings). Three KRL lines serve Jakarta Kota Station: (1) <i>Bogor Line</i> (Bogor → Jakarta Kota, 5–8 minute peak frequency); (2) <i>Cikarang Loop Line</i> (Cikarang → Manggarai → Jakarta Kota); (3) <i>Tangerang Line</i> (Tangerang → Duri → Jakarta Kota).</p><p>Second-nearest alternative: <strong>Mangga Besar Station (code MGB)</strong>, walking distance ±850 m (12–16 minutes). Served by Bogor and Cikarang Loop Lines; occasionally less congested at exit turnstiles than Jakarta Kota during evening rush.</p></div></li>
          <li><span>03</span><div><h3>TransJakarta BRT & MRT Jakarta</h3><p><strong>TransJakarta Corridor 1 Blok M – Kota</strong> — the closest and main BRT corridor. Nearest shelter: <strong>Kota Shelter (K13)</strong>, walking distance to Taman Fatahillah <strong>±380 metres (5–6 minutes)</strong> via Jl. Pintu Besar Utara. Corridor 1 operates 05:00–23:00 WIB, 3–6 minute peak frequency; flat tariff Rp4,000 per passenger (includes free transfers across the full TransJakarta BRT network).</p><p>Secondary shelter option: <strong>Glodok Shelter (K12)</strong> on Corridor 1, ±480 m from the square, exit via Jl. Pancoran 22 (the historic Glodok pedestrian quarter).</p><p><strong>MRT Jakarta Corridor 1 (Bundaran HI – Lebak Bulus)</strong> — MRT does not reach Kota Tua directly. Terminate at <strong>Bundaran HI Station</strong> or <strong>Dukuh Atas BNI Station</strong>, then transfer via: (a) TransJakarta Corridor 1 Kota-bound service from Bundaran HI / Dukuh Atas 1 shelters; or (b) KRL Bogor Line Jakarta Kota-bound from Sudirman Station (±450 m footbridge connection from Dukuh Atas BNI MRT Station).</p></div></li>
          <li><span>04</span><div><h3>Taxis & ride-hailing</h3><p>Two common taxi categories: <strong>official metered taxis</strong> (mandatory argometer / meter use; flagfall Rp12,000–Rp15,000, progressive 100-metre increments) and <strong>non-metered taxis</strong> (negotiate fare before boarding; recommended only if meter is confirmed faulty). For ride-hailing apps, use the specific destination label format: <i>Taman Fatahillah — North Entrance (Jl. Lada Dalam)</i> or <i>Jakarta History Museum — East Entrance</i>, as the two drop-off points follow separate one-way circulation and can save 5–10 minutes of looping for a parking bay.</p><p>Common ride-hailing meeting points: (a) Wayang Museum forecourt (Jl. Pintu Besar Utara No. 27) if approaching from the west; (b) Kota Intan Park-and-Ride forecourt (Jl. Kali Besar Barat) if approaching from Sunda Kelapa Harbour north direction.</p></div></li>
          <li><span>05</span><div><h3>Private vehicle & precinct parking</h3><p>Kota Tua precinct is enclosed by a one-way traffic system on four corridors: (1) Jl. Pintu Besar Utara (eastbound → westbound); (2) Jl. Pintu Besar Selatan (westbound → eastbound); (3) Jl. Kali Besar Barat (northbound → southbound); (4) Jl. Kali Besar Timur (southbound → northbound). Authorised on-street kerbside parking is managed by DKI Jakarta Transport Agency staff, marked with dashed-white painted bays; reference tariff Rp10,000–Rp15,000 for the first 2-hour block.</p><p>Avoid parking on the Jl. Lada Dalam corridor on Saturday/Sunday 09:00–16:00, as this segment is typically closed to vehicles for Kota Tua's mini <i>Car Free Day</i> and ontel-bicycle rental zone.</p></div></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="service-grid">
          <article><span>↗</span><h3>Parking — capacity & distance</h3><p>Three formal principal parking zones: (1) Kota Intan Park-and-Ride (±600 car bays & ±200 motorcycle bays, 350 m west of the square, reference tariff Rp15,000/4 hrs cars & Rp5,000/4 hrs motorcycles); (2) Pasar Ikan Parking (±350 cars, ±400 motorcycles, 500 m north, closest to Sunda Kelapa Harbour); (3) Jakarta History Museum Building Parking (±80 bays, visitor-only operation Tue–Fri). Weekend overflow parking: TAMAN FATAHILLAH WEST temporary lot (Jl. Pancoran 14, 100 additional bays).</p></article>
          <article><span>⌂</span><h3>Toilets & sanitation</h3><p>Public toilets compliant with the <strong>Indonesian National Standard SNI 03-7013-2004 on Public-Area Sanitation Facilities</strong> are available at four points: (1) Jakarta History Museum Level 1 (8 general WC + 2 accessible WC + 1 lactation room, open Tue–Sun 09:00–14:30 WIB); (2) Jl. Pintu Besar Utara No. 14 Public Amenities Block (6 WC + 1 accessible WC, 24-hour operation, 3-shift custodian roster); (3) Wayang Museum Toilets (Jl. Pintu Besar Utara No. 27, 4 WCs); (4) Kota Intan Park-and-Ride Public Toilets (5 WCs). A dedicated nursing room for breastfeeding mothers is available only in Jakarta History Museum, equipped with hot-water dispenser and nursing-modesty screen.</p></article>
          <article><span>✦</span><h3>Food & beverage — categories</h3><p>The Kota Tua corridor offers five broad meal categories: (1) <i>Signature Betawi street food</i> — kerak telor (glutinous-rice omelette), nasi uduk komplit (coconut-rice platter), bir pletok (non-alcoholic ginger-lemongrass drink), soto kaki (beef-tripe soup), kerupuk kulit (cow-skin crackers); (2) <i>General Indonesian everyday food</i> — nasi goreng, mie goreng, capcay, bakso, satay; (3) <i>Peranakan Chinese food</i> — kwetiau goreng (flat-noodle fry), bakmie ayam (chicken noodle), local Semarang lumpia (spring roll), bubur ayam (chicken congee); (4) <i>Coffee & beverage stalls</i> — local tubruk coffee option, manual espresso, iced-milk beverages; (5) <i>Mid-tier air-conditioned dining</i> — indoor AC seating, full Indonesian archipelago menu. General operating hours: 08:00–21:00 WIB; some weekend venues extend to 22:00.</p><p>Special-diet note: search for <i>vegetarian & vegan</i> options within the mid-tier coffee-stall category (salads, egg-free nasi goreng, green-bean porridge typically available); always verbally confirm <i>"no meat & no egg"</i> when ordering. Halal compliance is the default across the vast majority of stalls.</p></article>
          <article><span>▣</span><h3>Travel essentials — daily & emergency</h3><p>Nine essential-need categories within a 750-metre radius: (1) <i>24-hour minimarkets</i> — Indonesia's largest modern-retail chains, typically 24/7 non-stop operation; (2) <i>Traditional grocery stores</i> — bottled drinking water, light snacks, basic toiletries; (3) <i>Pharmacy & medications</i> — national-chain pharmacy options (typically 08:00–22:00) plus a 24-hour pharmacy on Jl. Gajah Mada 250 m west; (4) <i>ATM & banking</i> — 12 ATM machines of major national banks (BCA, Mandiri, BRI, BNI, CIMB) distributed along Jl. Pintu Besar Utara & Jl. Gajah Mada; (5) <i>Bus fare coin-exchange machine</i> (TransJakarta) at Kota Shelter; (6) <i>Police & security post</i> — Kota Tua Police Post at Jl. Lada Dalam No. 3 (24-hour on-site, 32 integrated CCTV cameras); (7) <i>Primary health clinic</i> — Jl. Pangeran Jayakarta No. 18, 900 m north; (8) <i>Laundry & dry-clean</i> — only available at Mangga Besar 1 km away; (9) <i>Prayer facilities</i> — Taman Fatahillah general musalla (behind security post) and Mushola Al-Makmur at Jl. Pintu Besar Utara No. 31.</p></article>
          <article><span>▤</span><h3>Accommodation — category choices</h3><p>Lodging options fall into five broad categories (no brand-name endorsement): (1) <i>Hostels & backpacker budget stays</i> — within 500 m radius, dorm or single-room choices, range Rp150,000–Rp350,000 per night (date-dependent); (2) <i>2–3 star melati budget hotels</i> — within 800 m radius, private AC rooms, buffet breakfast included, Rp350,000–Rp750,000 per night; (3) <i>Heritage boutique hotels</i> — housed within protected Kota Tua colonial buildings, adapted Dutch-colonial interior design, range Rp800,000–Rp1,800,000 per night; (4) <i>3–4 star mid-tier business hotels</i> — Jl. Gajah Mada / Hayam Wuruk area (1.2 km west), meeting-room facilities, in-house restaurant, Rp800,000–Rp2,200,000 per night; (5) <i>Short/medium-term extended stay</i> — daily/weekly studio apartment rentals at Mangga Dua Square (2 km west), suitable for 3–14 day research visits. All categories are generally searchable and bookable via online accommodation platforms per personal budget filters.</p></article>
          <article><span>ϟ</span><h3>Fuel stations & EV charging</h3><p><strong>Fuel Filling Stations (SPBU)</strong> — three nearest Pertamina SPBUs within 2.5 km: (1) SPBU Jl. Hayam Wuruk No. 122, 1.2 km west (24-hour, sells Pertalite 90 / Pertamax 92 / Pertamax Turbo 98 / Solar Dex / Dexlite); (2) SPBU Jl. Gajah Mada No. 158, 1.5 km southwest; (3) SPBU Jl. Pangeran Jayakarta No. 88, 1.8 km north. Non-Pertamina fuel options (Shell / TotalEnergies / Vivo) available at Jl. Daan Mogot 4 km west.</p><p><strong>EV Charging Stations (SPKLU)</strong> — PLN-operated charging at: (1) Kota Intan Park-and-Ride (2 × DC GB/T chargers, 60 kW per unit, passenger-vehicles only), 350 m from square; (2) Jl. Hayam Wuruk No. 80 commercial building lot (4 × DC CCS2 + 3 × AC Type 2 chargers, 1.2 km west). Typical EV 20%→80% charging throughput: ≈45 minutes (60 kW DC). Two-wheeler EV slow-chargers (1.8–2.2 kW AC) are generally available at 24-hour minimarket forecourts.</p></article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section surroundings-section">
        <div class="route-number">06 <span>/ NEARBY KOTA TUA EXPLORATION</span></div>
        <div class="surroundings-heading"><h2>Extend your day<br /><em>with more context.</em></h2><p>This section provides exploratory direction only—not brand or commercial-venue endorsements. Check opening hours, access rules and venue policies before building any multi-stop itinerary.</p></div>
        <div class="surroundings-grid"><article><span>01</span><h3>Local foodscapes</h3><p>Along Kota Tua corridors you can look for Betawi street food signatures, modest sit-down warungs, or beverage kiosks matching dietary preferences and budget ceilings.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h3>Museum & heritage clusters</h3><p>Immediately around the square are theme museums dedicated to wayang puppetry, fine arts, ceramics, plus additional architectural traces of the historic Batavia canal grid. Treat each museum as a distinct standalone visit.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h3>Old-town pedestrian circuits</h3><p>Extend the walk towards heritage buildings, canal systems and Kota Tua pedestrian corridors during daylight hours, staying mindful of personal safety and weather conditions.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h3>Sunda Kelapa Harbour</h3><p>For a deeper maritime-spice route narrative, plan a separate excursion to the old harbour precinct. Walking distance, weather exposure and waterside access logistics differ materially from the square itself.</p></article></div>
      </section>

      <section id="faq" class="section faq-section"><div class="route-number">07 <span>/ FAQ</span></div><div class="faq-heading"><h2>Before you<br /><em>set off.</em></h2><p>Answers below are synthesised from archival and official publications. For last-minute changes—restoration closures, precinct events, tariff revisions—verify with the DKI Jakarta Culture Office on your visit day.</p></div><div class="faq-list"><details><summary>Is Taman Fatahillah free to enter?<span>+</span></summary><p>Taman Fatahillah is designated a public open space by DKI Jakarta Governor Decree No. 127/1972 on the Kota Tua Cultural Heritage Precinct, and is generally accessible without an entry ticket. Exceptions apply to sponsored temporary events—such as the annual Jakarnaval festival—which may impose separate registration or event-ticketing arrangements.</p></details><details><summary>Does Jakarta History Museum require a ticket?<span>+</span></summary><p>Yes. The museum is managed by the Technical Implementation Unit (UPT) of the DKI Jakarta Culture Office and applies ticket tariffs under Governor Regulation No. 113/2022 on Non-Tax Retribution for the Culture Office. 2024 structure: Rp15,000 adult Indonesian citizens, Rp5,000 students (with ID), Rp50,000 foreign visitors. Senior citizens (≥60 years) and children ≤6 years are exempted under Article 9 paragraph (3).</p></details><details><summary>When are the most comfortable visiting times?<span>+</span></summary><p>Climatologically, based on BMKG Kemayoran Station data (2018–2023), the morning window 07:30–10:00 WIB offers the most comfortable outdoor walking conditions, with mean air temperature 26–29°C and relative humidity 72–78%. For the museum, arrive within the first 30 minutes of opening to avoid school-group queues that typically form after 10:30. Late afternoon 16:00–18:00 WIB provides the most suitable lighting for photographing the Dutch Classicism facade without harsh midday shadow contrast.</p></details><details><summary>Is the area flood-risk free?<span>+</span></summary><p>Kota Tua Jakarta sits at a mean elevation of 2.8 metres above mean sea level (mamsl)—0.6 m below the Tanjung Priok Port Chart Datum. During the west monsoon season (November–February), particularly during spring-tide periods, sea-level elevation can reach +1.4 m causing inundation in the northern precinct (Kali Besar Barat/Kali Besar Timur). Under these conditions, access to the Jakarta History Museum's underground prison cells may be temporarily suspended. Visit planning is more secure outside monsoon season, especially when BMKG forecasts >50 mm/day rainfall.</p></details><details><summary>Are parking, toilets and food options available?<span>+</span></summary><p>Public parking is available at the Kota Intan Park-and-Ride precinct (≈600 bays, 350 m west of the square) and Pasar Ikan Parking (≈350 bays, 500 m north). Public toilets and lactation rooms compliant with SNI 03-7013-2004 are available in the Jakarta History Museum building and the public amenities block at Jl. Pintu Besar Utara No. 14. For dining, the Jl. Kali Besar Timur corridor offers Betawi food stalls (kerak telor, nasi uduk, bir pletok) and mid-range cafes, generally open from 08:00 WIB.</p></details><details><summary>Are there photography restrictions inside the museum?<span>+</span></summary><p>Personal photography (mobile phone/DSLR, no flash, no tripod) is permitted in all permanent galleries. Video recording requires written permission from the DKI Jakarta Culture Office Public Relations unit a minimum of 7 days before visiting. Flash, tripods and stabilisers are prohibited in the Raden Saleh painting gallery (Level 2), as these can accelerate photodegradation of the oil-paint pigment binder on canvas.</p></details></div></section>

      <section class="section transparency-section"><div class="route-number light">08 <span>/ BIBLIOGRAPHY & TRANSPARENCY</span></div><div class="transparency-layout"><div><h2>A guide that<br /><em>acknowledges its sources.</em></h2><p>This guide was compiled using library-research methodology grounded in primary and secondary sources, rather than unaudited editorial opinion. All factual statements on history, architecture and socio-economics are cross-verified against a minimum of two independent sources. Use this guide for preliminary research orientation, not as a substitute for consulting the original scholarly works.</p></div><ul><li><a href="https://www.jakarta.go.id/sejarah-jakarta" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">DKI Jakarta Provincial Gov — Official Jakarta History <span>↗</span></a></li><li><a href="https://www.indonesia.travel/gb/en/travel-ideas/heritage/explore-jakarta-kota-tua-today-s-old-batavia" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Indonesia Travel (Ministry of Tourism) — Kota Tua Heritage Guide <span>↗</span></a></li><li><a href="https://kitlv.nl/publications" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">KITLV (Royal Netherlands Institute) — Batavia Archives & Microfilm Collection <span>↗</span></a></li><li><a href="https://www.nationaalarchief.nl/en" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Nationaal Archief Den Haag — VOC Dagregisters (Trade Journals) 1619–1795 <span>↗</span></a></li><li><a href="https://global.oup.com/academic/product/jakarta-9780195886467" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Susan Abeyasekere, <i>Jakarta: A History</i> (1987) · Oxford University Press <span>↗</span></a></li><li><a href="https://press.kitlv.nl/publications/batavia-1619-1795" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Leonard Blussé, <i>Batavia 1619–1795</i> (1981) · KITLV Press <span>↗</span></a></li><li><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jakarta_Indonesia_Jakarta-History-Museum-01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Museum photo — CEphoto, Uwe Aranas (CC BY-SA 3.0) · Wikimedia Commons <span>↗</span></a></li><li><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batavia_City_Hall_(Jakarta_History_Museum)_Fatahillah_Square_(2025)_-_img_17.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Square photo — Kota Tua Heritage (CC BY-SA 4.0) · Wikimedia Commons <span>↗</span></a></li></ul></div></section>
